  "live_records": {
    "q-39686": {
      "dom_id": "step-q39686",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Solve $\\sqrt{-3x-8}=x+2.$",
      "worked_solution": "To solve the equation $\\sqrt{-3x-8}=x+2,$ we raise both sides to the power of $2.$  This allows us to get rid of the radical, and gives us a quadratic equation that is easier to solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{-3x-8} & =x+2 \\\\ {(\\sqrt{-3x-8})}^{2} & =(x+2)^{2} \\\\ -3x-8 & ={x}^{2}+4x+4 \\\\ {x}^{2}+7x+12 & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}+4x)+(3x+12) & =0 \\\\ x(x+4)+3(x+4) & =0 \\\\ (x+3)(x+4) & =0 \\\\ x & =-4,\\,-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRemember that in square root equations, we should always check the obtained value(s) to ensure that they are actually solutions of the original equation.\n\n- First, we check whether the obtained value $x=-4$ is a solution of the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{-3(-4)-8} & \\overset{?}{=}-4+2 \\\\ \\sqrt{4} & \\overset{?}{=}-2 \\\\ 2 & \\ne -2\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe value $x=-4$ does *not* satisfy the original equation, so we discard it.\n\n- Second, we check whether the obtained value $x=-3$ is a solution of the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{-3(-3)-8} & \\overset{?}{=}-3+2 \\\\ \\sqrt{1} & \\overset{?}{=}-1 \\\\ 1 & \\ne -1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe value $x=-3$ does *not* satisfy the original equation, so we discard it.\n\nTherefore, there is no solution to $\\sqrt{-3x-8}=x+2x.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
