      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Solve $\\sqrt{2-3x}=2.$",
      "worked_solution": "Squaring both sides of the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2-3x} & =2 \\\\ {(\\sqrt{2-3x})}^{2} & ={2}^{2} \\\\ 2-3x & =4 \\\\ 3x & =-2 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check the result by plugging it into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2-3(-\\frac{2}{3})} & \\overset{?}{=}2 \\\\ \\sqrt{2-\\cancel{3}(-\\frac{2}{\\cancel{3}})} & \\overset{?}{=}2 \\\\ \\sqrt{2-(-2)} & \\overset{?}{=}2 \\\\ \\sqrt{2+2} & \\overset{?}{=}2 \\\\ \\sqrt{4} & \\overset{?}{=}2 \\\\ 2 & =2\\,\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe value $x=-\\frac{2}{3}$ satisfies the original equation, so we can accept it as a solution.\n\nTherefore, the solution to $\\sqrt{2-3x}=2$  is $x=-\\frac{2}{3}.$",
